 "prompt": "You are answering a biomedical research question.\n\nQuestion: Do inhaled corticosteroids affect perception of dyspnea during bronchoconstriction in asthma?\n\nAnswer \"yes\", \"no\", or \"maybe\", and give your confidence from 0 to 100 that your answer is correct.\n\nRespond with only a JSON object and no other text, in exactly this format:\n{\"answer\": \"yes\" or \"no\" or \"maybe\", \"confidence\": integer from 0 to 100}",
 "session_persisted": false,
 "text": "\n\n<｜DSML｜function_calls\n\n<｜DSML｜function_callsBased on my biomedical knowledge:\n\nInhaled corticosteroids are anti-inflammatory medications used to control asthma symptoms. They work by reducing airway inflammation but do not provide immediate bronchodilation. During acute bronchoconstriction, dyspnea (shortness of breath) perception is primarily related to mechanical airway obstruction and respiratory muscle loading. Corticosteroids may have some indirect effects on dyspnea perception through their anti-inflammatory actions, but they don't directly affect the neural pathways involved in dyspnea perception. However, by reducing airway inflammation over time, they could potentially influence dyspnea perception indirectly.\n\nGiven this, inhaled corticosteroids likely have minimal direct effect on the perception of dyspnea during acute bronchoconstriction episodes in asthma.\n\n{\"answer\": \"maybe\", \"confidence\": 65}",
